# PINGO — web environment
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values from your Supabase project:
#   Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API
#
#   cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Never commit real credentials.
#
# Note on the anon key: it is safe to expose in a browser bundle by design —
# it only grants what Row Level Security policies allow. The service_role key
# is NOT safe and must never appear in this file or anywhere in apps/web.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Google Sign-In on Android.
#
# This is the **Web** OAuth client ID, not the Android one — and that trips
# everybody up once. The Android client authorises the app itself, matched on
# package name and signing certificate; it holds no secret and is never named
# in code. But Supabase only accepts an ID token issued for *its* client, so
# the native sign-in asks Google for a token addressed to the web client and
# delivers it to an app already proven to be ours.
#
# Google Cloud → APIs & Services → Credentials → the "Web application" client.
# It must be the same one configured under Supabase → Authentication → Google.
#
# Web builds ignore this entirely; they use the OAuth redirect.
VITE_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=

# Turning a platform link into the video itself. Optional.
#
# Leave this empty and a YouTube or Instagram link renders as a card that plays
# the platform's own embed — which is what every install does by default and
# needs no infrastructure at all.
#
# Set it to a service that takes `{"url": "..."}` and answers with a direct
# media URL (the cobalt request shape), and pressing play fetches the real
# video instead: PINGO's own player, and a Save that writes it to the device.
#
# Two things worth being clear about before setting it:
#
#   - Nothing in this repository extracts video. PINGO only asks whatever
#     address is named here. Running such a service — and accepting the terms
#     of the platforms it fetches from — is a deployment decision, not
#     something the app does on its own.
#   - It must be a host you control. Every link anybody presses play on is sent
#     to it, so a public instance would be handing a stranger a feed of what
#     this group watches.
#
# Only `https://` is accepted; anything else is ignored as if unset.
VITE_MEDIA_RESOLVER_URL=
